 { Now let's see this theme being applied in practice. } ( { Endings with opposite-coloured bishops often feature the idea of one single diagonal. To start with, White should deal with the threat of } 1... d2 ) 
1. { -KEY- } Be3 $1 { An ideal diagonal for the bishop, where it controls the d3-pawn and will protect the own h-pawn later on. } ( { In contrast, after } 1. Ke3 $2 Bc4 2. Kd2 ( { or } 2. h5 $2 Kg7 { and the h-pawn falls } ) 
Kg6 { , it is Black who carries out the idea of one single diagonal. There can follow } 3. Be7 Kh5 4. Kc3 Ba6 5. Bg5 Kg4 6. Kb4 d2 $1 7. Bxd2 Kxh4 { with a draw - Mozetic. } ) 
Ke6 { Heading for the a-pawn. } ( { a) After } 1... Kg6 2. a6 Kh5 3. a7 Bd5 4. Bg5 Kg4 ( { or } 4... d2 5. Ke2 $1 ) 
5. Ke3 { contrary to the previous note, here Black's bishop can't control the a-pawn and protect the d-pawn at the same time, so he is lost. } ) 
 ( { b) As for } 1... Bc4 { after } 2. h5 Kf6 3. h6 { White also wins - Mozetic, by directing his king to the a-pawn. } ) 
2. a6 $1 { Another accurate move, preventing Black's king from dealing with the a-pawn. } ( { The natural } 2. h5 $2 { failed to } d2 $1 3. Bxd2 Kd7 { heading for a8, after which Black achieves a theoretical draw by sacrificing the bishop for the h-pawn - Mozetic. } ) 
Kf5 ( { Or } 2... Kd7 3. a7 Bd5 4. h5 Kc7 5. h6 Be4 6. h7 ) 
3. a7 Bd5 4. h5 Bc6 5. h6 ( { Black resigned, seeing that after the continuation } 5. h6 Kg6 6. Ke1 { , he can't prevent the transfer of the white king to the a-pawn. } ) 

 1-0

