 { Here is a similar example with more pawns. } 1. { -KEY- } b4 $1 ( { Fixing a target on a6 for the king. With hindsight, } 1. Be3 { first might have been more precise, and only after } Bf5 { (else White's king invades along d3-d4) the key move } 2. b4 { . } ) 
Bf5 { The best diagonal for this bishop. } ( { Black missed a subtle defensive idea here: } 1... Kh7 $1 2. Be3 d4 $1 3. cxd4 Bxa2 { , as observed by Farciarz. The point of this pawn trade is that White, contrary to the game, wouldn't be able to create a passed pawn on the queenside, while the d-pawn could be blocked by the bishop from d5. } ) 
2. Be5 Kh7 $6 { The king has no real future here. } ( 2... Kf8 3. Ke3 Ke7 4. Kd4 Ke6 { seems more logical, although after } 5. Bf4 Bxc2 6. h6 $1 { (preventing 6...Kf5) } Bg6 7. Kc5 Kf5 8. Be3 Ke4 9. Bd4 { White is much faster; e.g. } f5 10. Kb6 Kd3 11. Kxa6 Kc4 12. Ka5 $1 f4 13. a4 bxa4 14. Kxa4 f3 15. b5 { and wins. } ) 
3. Bf4 $1 { A key move: White parries the threat of 3...Kh6 and, for the longer term, installs his bishop on a diagonal where it can defend the h-pawn and control the enemy f-pawn at the same time. Interestingly, the bishop won't move again in this example! } Bg4 4. h6 Bf5 5. Ke3 $1 { Keres directs his king to a6, without bothering about the c2-pawn. } Bxc2 6. Kd4 Bb1 7. a3 { Of course that pawn should stay on the board. } Ba2 8. Kc5 Kg6 9. Kb6 Bc4 ( { Or } 9... Bb3 10. Kxa6 Ba4 11. Kb6 Kh7 12. Kc5 { and, after the capture on d5, one of the advances c3-c4 and a3-a4 decides. } ) 
10. Kxa6 Bd3 11. Ka5 $1 { Exploiting the last dynamic resource in the position, the a3-a4 advance. } Kf6 12. a4 bxa4 13. Kxa4 Ke6 14. Ka5 ( { If } 14. Ka5 Kd7 { , one simple option is } 15. b5 { followed by Kb6-c5, after which Black will be unable to defend both flanks. } ) 

 1-0

