 { Black is clearly much better, but in order to progress he must solve the problem of his weakness on e6, an issue which Tiviakov dealt with perfectly in the game. } 1... { -KEY- } Kb6 2. Bd7 d4 $1 3. exd4 Bd5 { It transpires that, on the a2-g8 diagonal, Black's bishop is confidently defending both of his key pawns. } 4. Ba4 Ka5 { The next step is to invade the enemy camp with the king. } 5. Bd1 Kb4 6. Bc2 h6 $1 { Good technique - in bishop endings it often makes sense to place one's pawns on squares of the opposite colour of the bishops. } 7. Bd1 ( { Or } 7. Bd3 a1=Q $1 8. Kxa1 Kc3 9. Bf1 Kd2 $1 { followed by ...Ke1xf2-e3 etc. } ) 
Kc4 { The rest is easy. } ( 7... a1=Q 8. Kxa1 Kc3 { also works, in similar fashion to the previous note. } ) 
8. Kxa2 Kxd4+ 9. Kb2 Ke4 10. Kc3 Kxf4 11. Kd4 g5 12. hxg5 hxg5 { Seeing that the plan of ...g5-g4-g3, followed by ...Kxg3 and ...f5-f4, can't be parried in any way, White resigned. } 
