 { Here is a final, complex example on the subject of one single diagonal.It is easy to conclude that White's only chance for a win is to transfer his king to the opposite flank, without losing the f-pawn in the process. In order to avoid the latter, he should not advance that pawn any further than f5, so that the bishop can protect it without losing track of the h-pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 ( { a) Let's examine two other tries: } 1. Kf3 Ke7 $1 2. Ke4 Kd6 { (preventing Kd5 so that White's king doesn't reach b7) } 3. Kf5 ( { or } 3. f5 Kc7 4. f6 Kb6 5. Bf1 Bf8 { with similar play } ) 
Kc7 4. Kg6 Kb6 5. Bf1 Bf8 { and, interestingly, it is now Black who applies the idea of one single diagonal, controlling the f-pawn and defending h6 at the same time. There can follow } 6. f5 ( { or } 6. Kf7 Bd6 7. f5 h5 $1 { and the h-pawn soon deflects the enemy bishop from the defence of the a6-pawn } ) 
Ka7 7. f6 Kb6 8. f7 Ka7 { and White can't progress without losing track of the h-pawn; e.g. } 9. Kh7 h5 10. Kg8 Bc5 11. f8=Q $11 Bxf8 12. Kxf8 h4 13. Ke7 h3 { with a draw - Ftacnik/Notkin. } ) 
 ( { b) } 1. Bf1 Ba7 ( { the tempting } 1. f5 h5 $2 { allows } 2. Kg3 $1 { focusing on the h5-pawn, e.g. } Ba7 3. Bd3 Kg7 4. Kh4 Kh6 5. Bf5 $1 { followed by a king transfer towards b7 } ) 
2. Kf3 Ke7 3. Ke4 h5 $1 { (only now, with further advance to h4 assured) } 4. Kf5 ( 4. Kd5 Kd7 5. Bh3 Kc7 { also holds } ) 
h4 $1 5. Kg4 Bf2 { and White can't progress. } ) 
Ba7 { Curiously, while making this excellent waiting move, Bacrot resigned! As the following analysis shows, Black can make a draw as long as he doesn't let White apply the idea of one single diagonal. } 2. Kf4 h5 $1 { The only saving idea, so as to avoid a position with the f-pawn on f7 and the white bishop ideally placed on the h5-e8 diagonal. } ( { Let's see this in concrete terms: } 2... Bg1 $2 3. Ke4 Ke7 4. Kd5 Kd7 5. Bg4 $1 Bf2 6. f6 Ke8 7. Bh5 $1 Kd7 8. f7 Ke7 9. Kc6 { and White wins. } ) 
 ( { A different move order with } 2... Ke7 3. Ke5 h5 $1 { also worked. } ) 
3. Ke4 Ke7 4. Kd5 Kd7 $1 { Preventing 5. Kc6. } ( { After } 4... Bg1 $2 5. Kc6 { followed by Kb7 and a6-a7, White wins thanks to the perfect location of his bishop, protecting the f-pawn and controlling the h-pawn at the same time. } ) 
5. f6+ { This is the only way to make progress, but as we already know, once the f-pawn leaves f5 it can no longer be comfortably defended by the bishop. } Ke8 6. Be6 h4 $1 { The pawn gets ready to sacrifice itself once the white king leaves the centre. } 7. Kc6 ( { After } 7. Kd6 Bg1 $1 ( { or any other waiting move; just not } 7. Kc6 h3 $2 8. Bxh3 Kf7 9. Ke5 ) 
8. Kc6 h3 $1 { Black draws in similar fashion to the main line. } ) 
h3 8. Kb7 ( { Or } 8. Bxh3 Kf7 ) 
h2 9. Bd5 Bd4 10. a7 Bxa7 11. Kxa7 h1=Q $1 12. Bxh1 Kf7 { Black draws - Ftacnik/Notkin. } 
 *
